## Risks

### RK-001 The backend chain decides the finish date

Severity: medium. Origin: llm.

CPM duration is 172 h (28.5 working days); 9 of 27 tasks (33%) are critical. Layer backend holds 112 of 172 critical-path hours (65%). Most of the longest path is backend work done one task after another, so a slip in any of those tasks moves the release date, and a second developer cannot shorten a chain of dependent tasks.

Mitigation: Name an owner per critical backend task, review designs before coding starts, and split the largest tasks so that frontend and testing work can begin on agreed API contracts while the backend is built.

Cited signals:

- SG-01 (critical_path_length, medium): CPM duration is 172 h (28.5 working days); 9 of 27 tasks (33%) are critical.
- SG-12 (critical_layer_concentration, medium): Layer backend holds 112 of 172 critical-path hours (65%).

### RK-002 Payment and order state work gates tracking, notifications and couriers

Severity: high. Origin: llm.

T-012 "Payment and order confirmation flow" has 9 downstream tasks and 0 h slack (CPM). T-014 "Order status state machine and live update stream" has 7 downstream tasks and 0 h slack (CPM). Both tasks sit on the critical path, and the payment provider's sandbox onboarding and webhook behaviour are outside the team's control.

Mitigation: Open the payment provider account and start its sandbox integration in the first sprint, agree the order status transitions early so dependent screens can use a stub, and keep a mock payment mode for demos.

Cited signals:

- SG-05 (critical_high_fanout, high): T-012 "Payment and order confirmation flow" has 9 downstream tasks and 0 h slack (CPM).
- SG-06 (critical_high_fanout, high): T-014 "Order status state machine and live update stream" has 7 downstream tasks and 0 h slack (CPM).

### RK-003 Early sprints have no spare capacity

Severity: high. Origin: llm.

Sprint 1 is loaded to 98% (39 of 40 points). Sprint 2 is loaded to 100% (40 of 40 points). A plan that is already full cannot absorb sick days, review rework or one underestimated task.

Mitigation: Move low-priority work out of the first two sprints, leave a deliberate buffer in sprint planning, and re-estimate after the first sprint using the real completed points.

Cited signals:

- SG-08 (sprint_overload, medium): Sprint 1 is loaded to 98% (39 of 40 points).
- SG-09 (sprint_overload, high): Sprint 2 is loaded to 100% (40 of 40 points).

### RK-004 Little testing before a late integration point

Severity: medium. Origin: llm.

Testing is 8 of 117 points (6.8%), below the 10% minimum. T-025 "End-to-end order journey tests" has 5 direct predecessors (integration bottleneck). Problems between the customer, restaurant and courier screens would first show up near the end, when little time is left to fix them.

Mitigation: Add unit and API tests to each backend task definition of done, add a payment-flow test task, and run a thin end-to-end journey as soon as checkout works rather than waiting for the last screen.

Cited signals:

- SG-10 (layer_imbalance, medium): Testing is 8 of 117 points (6.8%), below the 10% minimum.
- SG-07 (high_fan_in, medium): T-025 "End-to-end order journey tests" has 5 direct predecessors (integration bottleneck).

### RK-005 High-severity signal: critical_high_fanout

Severity: high. Origin: template.

T-002 "Restaurant and menu database schema" has 18 downstream tasks and 0 h slack (CPM).

Mitigation: Review this signal with the team, assign an owner and decide whether the task can be split or started earlier.

Cited signals:

- SG-02 (critical_high_fanout, high): T-002 "Restaurant and menu database schema" has 18 downstream tasks and 0 h slack (CPM).

